  "cause": "**Spain's transport minister resigned six days after the second fatal crash on the same high-speed line in under a year.** **Óscar Puente** announced in Congress on Tuesday evening, after giving evidence on the **28 October** disaster near **Urda**, south of Madrid, in which **at least 69 people** were killed and dozens injured — a **Renfe** train with **397 passengers** running into the back of a stationary **Iryo** train carrying **147**. His own words set the standard he then met: *\"A transport minister in a country like Spain cannot remain in office after two accidents of this seriousness in less than a year\"* — a reference to **January's** crash on the same line, which killed **46**. He added that he was withdrawing his name from the lists for the **early general elections on 29 November**, and called the decision *\"political dignity\"*. Prime Minister **Pedro Sánchez** praised a colleague who *\"faced up to the situation, provided answers and solutions, and then stepped aside… Out of dignity. Of his own accord. Out of respect for the victims.\"* **What the recordings appear to show is a control-room failure, not a driver failure.** A reported issue with the **automatic signalling system — a computer failure — had forced all trains to halt** on the affected stretch. The **three recordings** of conversations between Madrid's control centre and two drivers appear to show the **Renfe train being given clearance to move while the other train stood on the track about 25km ahead**. In one, an operator is speaking to the Renfe driver, who was having technical difficulties. Puente had been criticised for appearing to attribute the crash to **human error** — which the opposition read as deflecting blame from a system whose credibility **Sánchez himself admitted was \"broken\"**. He had said days earlier that he would stay. **The political timing is not incidental.** The crash landed less than four weeks before a general election the governing Socialists were already expected to lose; the **PP** and **Vox**, and some government allies, had called for his head; and the resignation now removes a minister but not the question of who answers for a signalling system that halted every train and then cleared one into a stationary one.",
